WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:01.900
- This episode of "LegalEagle"

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.500
was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.050
With the passing of Ruth Bader Ginsburg,

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.260
President Trump was able
to nominate and appoint

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.510
his third Supreme Court
justice, Amy Coney Barrett.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.010
This rankled many Democrats

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.380
because the process took place

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.070
right before the 2020 election

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.160
and under COVID circumstances.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.380
And the process included a
COVID superspreader event

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:21.213
at the White House.

00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:22.790
But they accused President Trump

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.310
and Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.760
of rank hypocrisy, given
that the Republican Senate

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.860
refused to hold a hearing

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.230
on President Obama's
pick for Supreme Court,

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.063
Merrick Garland,

00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:35.210
who was nominated 237 days
before the 2016 election.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.860
- I think it's safe to say

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.510
there will not be hearings or votes.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.760
I think it is also safe to say

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.860
the next president, whoever that may be,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.880
is gonna be the person who chooses

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.350
the next Supreme Court justice.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.900
- This appointment of Amy Coney
Barrett to the Supreme Court

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.240
represents a large shift
in ideology on the Court

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.620
and some Democrats are
calling for retribution.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.960
And this has caused some to suggest

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.090
that if President Biden

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.560
is able to take control of the Senate,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.120
he should engage in what's
called court packing.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.760
If the Democrats are able to take Georgia,

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.150
they could conceivably
have the votes to do it.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.700
And the thing is, there's nothing special

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.890
about the number of justices
on the Supreme Court.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.300
We didn't arrive at that
number by divine intervention

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.180
and it's not in the Constitution.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.700
So that raises the question,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.070
would it in fact be legal

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.220
to just simply expand the Supreme Court

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.480
if the Democrats take the Senate?

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.330
Can the party simply add
justices to the Supreme Court

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.940
and then fill those vacancies?

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:25.900
What happened the last time

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.690
a president tried to pack the Court?

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.140
And are there any other limits

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.225
that can be imposed on the
members of the Supreme Court?

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:35.530
(regal orchestral music)

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.530
Hey Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.220
because the future of the Supreme Court

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.760
is actually at stake.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.330
You might recall that two years ago,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.670
Marco Rubio introduced a
Constitutional amendment

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.330
limiting the Supreme Court
to nine justices forever.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.157
He said, "There's nothing
magical about the number nine.

00:01:50.157 --> 00:01:51.337
"It's not inherently right

00:01:51.337 --> 00:01:53.207
"just because the number of
seats on the Supreme Court

00:01:53.207 --> 00:01:55.877
"remains unchanged since 1869,

00:01:55.877 --> 00:01:57.857
"but there is something
inherently good and important

00:01:57.857 --> 00:02:00.007
"about preventing the
further destabilization

00:02:00.007 --> 00:02:01.720
"of essential institutions."

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.330
So is Senator Rubio correct

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.400
that in order to limits
on the Supreme Court

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.640
we'd actually need a
Constitutional amendment?

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.820
Well, it depends on what kind of limits

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.900
you're talking about.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.450
And to understand the Supreme Court,

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.460
we have to understand what's
called the Judiciary Act

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.190
and of course the US Constitution.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.530
The Constitution mandates
that there be a chief justice.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.910
Article I, Section III, Clause
6 refers to a chief justice,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.087
quote, "Who shall preside
over the impeachment trial

00:02:24.087 --> 00:02:25.520
"of the President of the United States."

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.240
But beyond that, the
Constitution is silent

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.450
as to how many justices actually
have to be on the Court.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.990
In theory, the Constitution
allows for just two justices,

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.370
or 11, 23, or 41.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.180
It doesn't say that there
is an optimal number

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.460
and that's for Congress to decide.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.600
Article III, Section 1 of the Constitution

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.970
gives Congress broad discretion

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.160
to establish the court system.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.207
Quote, "The judicial
Power of the United States

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:48.127
"shall be vested in one Supreme Court,

00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:50.277
"and in such inferior
Courts as the Congress

00:02:50.277 --> 00:02:53.060
"may from time to time
ordain and establish."

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.310
Now Article III does not
provide how many justices

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.000
will still on this Supreme Court.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.430
That was to be hashed out politically

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.450
by the earliest members of
Congress and the president.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.170
We typically call these
folks the Founding Fathers

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.340
to give them extra gravitas,

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.650
but at the end of the day,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.810
they were just the earliest
politicians in this country.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.020
They made some brilliant decisions

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.310
and some boneheaded ones,

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.680
and that's what happens when
you're inventing a new country.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.760
And the Judiciary Act of 1789

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.690
established the first Supreme
Court with six justices.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.130
The country was so small that
the Supreme Court justices,

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.620
quote, "Rode the circuit,"

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.090
hearing cases in the
regional circuit courts

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.480
as well as the cases
before the Supreme Court.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.440
But in 1801, President John Adams

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.390
and a lame duck Federalist Congress

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.660
passed the Judiciary Act of 1801

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.760
which actually reduced
the number of justices

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.000
on the Court to five.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.870
The goal was to stop incoming president

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.580
Thomas Jefferson's Court appointments.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.530
Jefferson and his Democratic Republicans

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.650
quickly repealed the act,

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.367
putting the Court back to six justices.

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:45.360
But President Jefferson wasn't convinced

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.390
that six was the right number of justices

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.640
for the Supreme Court either

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.720
and in 1807, Jefferson and Congress

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.900
agreed to expand the Court,
adding a seventh justice

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.910
when Congress added a
seventh federal circuit.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.160
The ideal being that as
the country gets bigger,

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.450
you need more federal
circuits and thus more judges

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.170
are needed for the Supreme Court as well.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.070
And the next amendment to
the Judiciary Act in 1837

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.390
followed this same principle.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.830
President Andrew Jackson
added two additional justices

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.080
after Congress expanded

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.650
the number of federal
circuit courts again.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.840
And the next big amendment
to the Judiciary Act

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:14.990
was during the Civil War

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.090
when Congress created
the 10th circuit in 1863.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.250
At this point, the Supreme
Court had 10 justices,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:23.790
but in 1866, after the war,
Congress passed legislation

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.270
to gradually reduce the
Court to seven justices.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:27.900
This happened for two reasons.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.760
First, Republicans were
redrawing the geographical lines

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.770
for the judicial circuits, going
back down to nine circuits.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.560
So why not nine justices then?

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.010
Well, because of the second point.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.530
They wanted to limit the influence

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.060
of the former Confederate states.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:40.893
There'd been a tradition

00:04:40.893 --> 00:04:42.640
of appointing a judge from each circuit,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.390
so each circuit had a justice

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.330
who could also hear circuit cases.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.510
But this meant that between 1837 and 1862

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:50.850
five of the nine circuits

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.910
comprised exclusively Slave states.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.750
So former Confederates still
had a disproportionate impact

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.280
on who was on the Supreme Court

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.670
watching out for their interest.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.320
But the 1866 acts left only two circuits

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.230
that comprised of only former Slave states

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.270
and only one that comprised
only former Confederate states.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.500
But as you learn from history,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.930
Democrats fought
Reconstruction tooth and nail

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.450
and three years later a new Judiciary Act

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.370
put the number back to nine justices.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.347
The Judiciary Act of 1869 said,

00:05:16.347 --> 00:05:18.957
"Be it enacted by the Senate
and House of Representatives

00:05:18.957 --> 00:05:20.987
"of the United States
in Congress assembled,

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:22.477
"that the Supreme Court
of the United States

00:05:22.477 --> 00:05:23.847
"shall here and after consist

00:05:23.847 --> 00:05:25.577
"of the Chief Justice of the United States

00:05:25.577 --> 00:05:27.297
"and eight associate justices,

00:05:27.297 --> 00:05:29.627
"any six of whom shall
constitute a quorum;

00:05:29.627 --> 00:05:31.507
"and for the purposes of this act

00:05:31.507 --> 00:05:32.577
"there shall be appointed

00:05:32.577 --> 00:05:35.200
"an additional associate
justice of said court."

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.490
So that more or less brings us up to date

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.350
with the Judiciary in 2020.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.820
The number of Supreme Court justices

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.180
has remained at nine since 1869.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.340
And there are currently 12
regional federal circuits

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.990
organized from the 94
US judicial districts

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:51.170
and there's a 13th circuit
that's called the Federal Circuit

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.450
which has nationwide
jurisdiction over certain appeals

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:54.600
based on subject matter.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:55.980
And, obviously, the US has done away

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.750
with the custom that there
should be one justice

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.330
for each of the judicial circuits,

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.920
otherwise we would have
four additional justices.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.300
But let's pause here and think back

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.780
to Senator Rubio's proposal

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.140
that there should be nine justices

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:07.610
on the Supreme Court forever.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.550
Why would that be?

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:09.810
Well, for comparison purposes,

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.410
consider other common law countries.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:13.780
The Supreme Court of the
United Kingdom has 12 members.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.370
Australia's highest court has seven.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:16.580
Canada's has nine.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:17.590
Does this really tell us anything

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:19.440
about the fairness of the institutions?

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.760
We could, of course,
make this a numbers game,

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:22.300
but that's not really what this all about.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.860
Supreme Court battles
are all about ideology.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.260
Whose views are represented

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.010
and what are the judicial philosophies

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:28.970
of the different justices,

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.740
and how does a judge tend to
rule about a particular issue?

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.590
As much as both sides try
to stand on principle,

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.690
the Court battles are mainly about power

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.250
and this is just as true now

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.180
as it historically has been over time.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:40.170
And for comparison's sake,

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.160
the population of the US back in 1869

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.140
was almost 39 million people.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:46.020
The population in the US in 2010

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.790
was over 300 million people.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.100
You could argue that a
nation of 300 million people

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.170
needs a bigger Supreme Court

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.400
to adequately represent
everyone's interests.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.210
And many countries in Europe ensure

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.320
that the courts have justices

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.270
representing all sides of
the political spectrum.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.477
So given this logic, the rising population

00:07:00.477 --> 00:07:02.970
and the fact that there is no requirement

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:04.260
that the number of justices

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:06.150
on the Supreme Court stay at nine,

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.420
and of course the tit-for-tat nature

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.370
of adding more justices in retribution

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:13.000
for what happened to Merrick Garland,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.470
some people have argued that
the next Democratic president

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.900
should simply add justices
to the Supreme Court,

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.330
something that's called court packing.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.890
Now many of you might be familiar

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.810
with the term court packing
from your school days.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.530
Because President Biden won't be the first

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:27.820
to wrestle with the idea of court packing.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:28.980
And what most people remember

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:32.300
is that FDR tried to add six
justices to the Supreme Court,

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.160
but that's not quite the whole story.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.630
In the midst of the Great Depression,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.170
Roosevelt had proposed a
number of laws and regulations

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:39.930
to stop the economic meltdown

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:41.640
and to provide welfare to Americans.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.500
Generally these are referred
to as FDR's New Deal.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:45.850
But the Supreme Court overturned

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.170
a number of FDR's New Deal programs

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.340
on the basis that they
were unconstitutional,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.550
saying generally that Congress
simply didn't have the power

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:56.500
to pass these kinds of
economic regulations

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:57.410
because they didn't have the power

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.630
under the Constitution's Commerce clause.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.080
And FDR particularly disagreed

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.360
with the decision the
Supreme Court made in 1935

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.880
in Schechter Poultry
Corp. versus United States

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:06.960
which struck down a part

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.890
of the National Industrial Recovery Act.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:10.210
And given that the Supreme Court

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.540
had said these laws were unconstitutional,

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.800
there was nothing that Congress
or the president could do.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:16.350
They lacked the power.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.820
It wasn't available
under the Constitution.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:19.860
So in retaliation,

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.080
Roosevelt suggested
expanding the membership

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:22.990
of the Supreme Court.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:25.640
The law would've added one
justice to the Supreme Court

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:27.640
for every justice over the age of 70,

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.030
with a maximum of six additional justices.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:31.760
The idea being that he
would pack the Court

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.600
with justices who agreed with his ideology

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:36.780
and his interpretation of the Constitution

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.200
that would allow Congress
and the president

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.280
to pass the New Deal legislation.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:42.820
But after public outcry,

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.730
the law to expand the Supreme
Court did not in fact pass.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.460
But interestingly, the Supreme Court

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.620
did take a more favorable
view of New Deal legislation

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:52.240
in allowing much of it to stand.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.150
And since that time,
it obviated the request

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.150
for more people to join the Supreme Court

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.080
and FDR's attempt to pack the courts

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.280
has been portrayed as a
power grab ever since.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.780
But there's an additional
dimension to FDR's plan

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.830
that often doesn't get recognition.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.510
It actually doesn't add,
necessarily, six justices.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:10.070
It would've simply added one justice

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:12.550
for every sitting justice
over the age of 70

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:14.030
up to a maximum of six,

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:16.310
ensuring that you would
never fall below nine,

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:18.360
even if the older judges died or retired.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.160
In part, FDR's plan was addressing

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.950
the lifetime tenue of
Supreme Court judgeships.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.510
Life terms for Supreme Court justices

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.400
has always been controversial.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:27.980
But in 1789, at the time

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:29.630
of the first Constitutional Convention,

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.930
life expectancy was 34 years old.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:35.230
But 2011, life expectancy
was 78 years old.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.840
Is this a problem for the Supreme Court?

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.890
Well, that depends on who you ask.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:40.880
At times, Congress passed legislation

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.710
to nudge judges towards retirement.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:45.240
Several Republican Congresses in the 1860s

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.800
provided full salaries and benefits

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.870
to all federal judges who retired.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:50.960
This worked and Republicans
were able to fill

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.560
several formerly Democratic
seats on the Judiciary.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:56.280
In 1932, Herbert Hoover
enacted austerity measures

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.113
during the Depression.

00:09:57.113 --> 00:09:58.770
One of them was cutting
Supreme Court justices

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:00.220
retirement salaries in half.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:01.990
According to research by Judge Glock,

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:03.750
Herbert Hoover's attorney general warned

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.617
that the law meant justices will, quote,

00:10:05.617 --> 00:10:06.927
"Hang on like grim death

00:10:06.927 --> 00:10:09.180
"until the Angel Gabriel blows the horn."

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.600
He was, of course, correct.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.720
Once the bill passed,
nobody wanted to retire.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:13.960
FDR recognized this

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.420
and attempted to restore their
full retirement salaries,

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.380
but the bill failed.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:18.800
And that was actually part

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:20.710
of FDR's court packing plan as well.

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.000
The legislation would've restored

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.330
the justices' full pensions.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:24.840
Judge Glock's research on this matter

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:26.440
found records in FDR's library,

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.260
in the papers of his attorney
general, Homer Cummings,

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:30.770
demonstrating that the
Court expansion plan

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.420
was modeled on what
Republicans did in the 1960s.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.900
And in 1937, Congress did, in fact,

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.120
restore the full pensions
for retiring justices

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.920
and one justice actually retired.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.980
Now, critics of court packing point out

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:44.830
some extremely obvious
flaws with the idea.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.170
The first of which is that court packing

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:47.900
isn't really a permanent solution.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:51.710
Since this isn't codified
in the Constitution itself,

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:56.200
if the norm of a nine justice
Supreme Court is violated,

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.470
then effectively any
time a political party

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:02.770
takes control of the House,
the Senate and the presidency,

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:04.640
then they can change the number

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.320
of justices on the Supreme Court.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.510
And the two parties can enter
into a never-ending cycle

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.550
of one-upsmanship where
they simply expand the Court

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.930
whenever it is convenient for them.

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:16.840
And in fact, former Harvard professor

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:18.597
and legal commentator Lawrence Tribe says,

00:11:18.597 --> 00:11:20.357
"Obviously partisan Court-expansion

00:11:20.357 --> 00:11:23.637
"to negate the votes of justices
whose views a party detests

00:11:23.637 --> 00:11:25.317
"and whose legitimacy the party doubts

00:11:25.317 --> 00:11:27.167
"could trigger a tit-for-tat spiral

00:11:27.167 --> 00:11:29.557
"that would endanger the
Supreme Court's vital role

00:11:29.557 --> 00:11:32.220
"in stabilizing the national
political and legal system."

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:34.810
So not only could the Supreme
Court expand and expand

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:36.130
any time that a political party

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:38.070
finds it convenient to do so,

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.790
but that very expansion might take away

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.010
some of the legitimacy
that people associate

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:46.460
with the Supreme Court's
rulings on different things

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:50.020
and remove some of the independence

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.550
that the Supreme Court is,
at least theoretically,

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:53.640
supposed to have.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.480
So apart from simply adding
justices to the Supreme Court,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.080
what else could be done to reform it?

00:11:58.080 --> 00:11:59.770
Well, some people are arguing

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.760
for ending lifetime appointments.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:03.830
This is Constitutionally suspect.

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:06.410
The Constitution says that the
judges of the Supreme Court

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:08.620
will stay in times of good behavior,

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.370
which generally has been interpreted

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:11.910
as being a lifetime appointment,

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:15.970
that the judge gets to
decide when he or she retires

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.830
and that there's nothing
that Congress can do

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:20.480
to force someone to early retirement

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.740
or to set term limits for the justices.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.770
And there are many
places in federal service

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.340
where you want lifetime appointments

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:30.990
to be free from any kind
of political influence.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.270
But some scholars suggest
that you can get around this

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.150
by simply rotating the judges through,

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:38.390
that someone can be appointed
to a term in the Supreme Court

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:40.740
and then relegated to one
of the federal circuits

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.430
after a certain number of
years have been served.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.290
And there are pros and
cons to this approach.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.580
Some argue that having
a lifetime appointment

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:48.450
means that justices

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.850
will be completely free
from political influence.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.420
Others say that it simply
creates an incentive

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:56.580
for politicians to appoint
extremely young judges

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.410
who agree with you ideologically,

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.940
who will stay on the Court
for years and years and years

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:03.780
and then the other side simply
won't have an opportunity

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.350
to appoint somebody else.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:07.480
But as we saw since the FDR days,

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.710
a lifetime appointment means
that some of the justices

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.590
will simply hold on forever,

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:14.200
until they actually might
pass away in office.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.140
This is one of those areas

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.210
that regardless of what
the Constitution says,

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:18.860
there's not necessarily a right answer

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:21.800
in terms of term limits
for Supreme Court justices.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.330
In fact, Justice Ginsburg
supported shorter terms

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:25.880
for Supreme Court justices.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.060
The most popular suggestion

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.530
is limiting justices to 18 year terms.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:30.630
This is a reform that seems

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.360
to have had bipartisan
support in the past,

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.670
but there are some
Constitutional problems with it.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.840
Neither Congress nor the
president can fire judges

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.630
or force them out of office without cause,

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:40.920
however the Constitution provides

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:44.460
that justices shall hold their
offices during good behavior.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:45.910
Good behavior has been interpreted

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:48.000
as conferring lifetime tenue on judges

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.310
and imposing term limits

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.570
would probably require a
Constitutional amendment.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:53.060
Though some argue it's possible

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.880
that you could shuffle
a Supreme Court justice

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.580
off of the Supreme Court into
one of the federal circuits,

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:02.120
thus keeping them employed in
the federal system as a judge,

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.460
just not on the Supreme
Court for their life tenue.

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:08.250
But this also wouldn't solve
the politicization problem.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:11.530
Battles over nominees would
probably still be fierce.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:14.680
Some argue for a bigger Court
and a panel review system.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:17.260
Vanderbilt professors Tracey
George and Chris Guthrie

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:18.910
propose that Congress change

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:20.250
the Court's institutional design.

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:22.390
Their idea is to increase the size

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:23.470
of the Supreme Court's membership

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.360
and remake in the image of
the US court of appeals.

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:27.770
First, they suggest of
increasing the size,

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:30.150
so it's approximately the same
size as the circuit courts,

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.150
which have around 13 judges per circuit.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:33.670
Second, they propose that the Court

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:35.500
should hear almost all the cases in panels

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:36.730
rather than as a full Court,

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:38.840
which could at least double
and perhaps even triple

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:40.950
the Supreme Court's
decision making capacity,

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.490
which means that the Court
could decide even more cases.

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:44.960
Panels would be randomly selected,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:46.210
so we would not know in advance

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:48.470
precisely which judges
would decide the case.

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:49.830
And third, Guthrie and George

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.410
say that the Court should
retain the authority

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:54.360
to grant and en banc review
in the small fraction of cases

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.020
that call for the Court
to speak as a full body.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:58.960
And this is effectively how
the other federal circuits

00:14:58.960 --> 00:14:59.910
operate all the time.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:01.460
Theoretically, the
benefits of this proposal

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:03.700
include greater consistency
and clarity of in the law

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:05.130
in addition to, arguably,

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.630
less politicized nomination processes.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.670
And still others argue
for Supreme Court reforms

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.630
that acknowledge that it is,
in fact, a political body.

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:15.110
Some argue for what's called
the Balance Bench approach,

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:16.670
a plan created by law professors

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:18.800
Daniel Epps and Ganesh Sitaraman.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:19.760
Their plan in a nutshell

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.920
starts with 10 Supreme Court justices,

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:23.820
five would be affiliated
with the Democratic Party

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:25.440
and five with the Republican Party.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.010
These 10 justices would then
select five additional justices

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:29.530
chosen from the current circuit,

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.260
or possibly district court judges.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.510
These five circuit court judges

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.090
would need to be selected unanimously

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.230
by the 10 partisan judges

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.670
and they'd need to be
chosen two years in advance

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:39.720
for one year terms.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.100
And if the justices fail to agree

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:42.880
on a slate of additional colleagues,

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.540
the Supreme Court would lack a quorum

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:46.980
and they could not hear
any cases for that year.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:48.990
The Balanced Bench plan rests on the idea

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:50.870
that the partisan affiliated justices

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:52.190
would have to pick justices

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:54.020
would probably would never be
appointed to the Supreme Court

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:56.480
because they have views
that aren't easily traceable

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:57.840
to party affiliations.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.270
And Epps and Sitaraman
believe that the justices

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.637
would select five people who
have a reputation for, quote,

00:16:02.637 --> 00:16:05.030
"Fairness, independence, and centrism."

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:07.010
But of course, regardless
whatever benefits

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:10.160
this approach would have,
it's clearly unconstitutional,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.060
so it would require a
Constitutional amendment

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:13.310
to actually implement.

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:16.860
But why let a silly little thing
like being unconstitutional

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:18.870
prevent it from being adopted?

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:20.960
But there's no dispute
that, for better or worse,

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.560
the system currently is
based largely on luck.

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:25.620
Some presidents might not get to appoint

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:27.400
a single Supreme Court justice

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.810
while others might appoint three or four.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:31.540
But whether there are nine justices

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.520
on the Supreme Court or 900,

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:35.170
if you ever want to get
to the Supreme Court,

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:37.100
you've got to learn to write really well.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:39.030
In fact, all lawyers have
to write really well.

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.680
And the best place to learn
